# domains.txt — YOUR own / client assets to cross-reference against scan results.
# Copy this to domains.txt (gitignored) and fill in your real estate.
#
# One entry per line. Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored.
# The parser accepts SIX shapes (mix freely in one file):
#
#   1. bare domain / hostname   — matched exactly OR as a parent of any subdomain
#   2. subdomain                — matched exactly
#   3. bare IPv4 / IPv6         — matched by IP (stored as a /32 or /128)
#   4. CIDR range (v4 or v6)    — matched by IP containment
#   5. http:// or https:// URL  — the scheme is stripped, leaving the host
#   6. host with a /path        — the path is dropped, leaving the host
#
# How matching works against a harvested result:
#   - by NAME: your entry matches if a found host equals it, or is a subdomain
#     of it. So `example.com` also catches `shop.example.com`, `a.b.example.com`.
#   - by IP: a found host's IP is matched if it falls inside any CIDR/bare IP
#     you list. This catches hosts that surface with NO hostname (IP only).

# --- 1. bare domains / hostnames (most common) ---
example.com
client-site.co.id
# `example.com` above ALSO flags any subdomain Shodan returns, e.g. blog.example.com

# --- 2. explicit subdomains (when you only own that one label) ---
shop.example.com
mail.client-site.co.id

# --- 3. bare IPs (a host that shows up with no DNS name still gets flagged) ---
203.0.113.10
2001:db8:abcd::25

# --- 4. CIDR ranges (whole blocks you own — e.g. your Indonesian IP space) ---
203.0.113.0/24
198.51.100.0/22
2001:db8:abcd::/48

# --- 5. scheme-prefixed (paste straight from a browser; scheme is stripped) ---
https://portal.example.com
http://old.client-site.co.id

# --- 6. host with a path (path is dropped; only the host is used) ---
example.com/wp-admin
client-site.co.id/blog/

# Auto-populate domains from Certificate Transparency logs (the attacker's own method):
#   curl -s "https://crt.sh/?q=%25.example.com&output=json" \
#     | jq -r '.[].name_value' | sed 's/\*\.//' | sort -u >> domains.txt
#
# id.zone is already this shape — a list of Indonesia CIDR ranges (form 4).
#
# Then run, e.g.:
#   python3 scanner_dork.py --engine shodan --mine domains.txt --country ID
#   python3 scanner_dork.py --engine shodan --mine domains.txt --country ID --exploit --exploit-dry-run
